PSPATTERN 0.5.0a

    Tables have a transpose column. Sixteen rows of semitones on the
        left of the three command columns, shifting the note the table
        is running against. It is an offset rather than a new note, so
        an arpeggio running underneath arpeggiates around the
        transposed pitch instead of cancelling it, and a row left at
        -- changes nothing.

    Projects written before this load with the column at zero, and
        projects written after it open in an older build with the
        column ignored. Neither direction needs anything from you.

    Four new commands.

    CHRD abcd is a chord: the same four semitone offsets ARPG takes,
        always cycled at tick rate, so there is no ARPS to set as
        well. The channels are monophonic, so this is what a chord can
        be.

    RTGR aabb is the shape of a retrigger: aa is the volume change per
        repeat and bb the pitch change in semitones, both signed. Put
        it in the other command column beside RTRG. Synth only.

    RAND aabb scatters the note it sits beside, aa semitones of pitch
        either way and bb of level. Level only ever goes down, because
        a command that could make a step louder than the one you set
        is a command that clips a mix you had balanced. Synth only.

    TRSP --bb shifts the note by bb semitones and holds it there. This
        is what the table's transpose column emits, and it is useful
        typed into a step on its own.

    Opening a project left tables 08 to 7F holding whatever the last
        project had in them. Only the first eight were being cleared.

    The website has a plain list of everything the program does.

PSPATTERN 0.4.0a

    Four palettes instead of one, picked by a single line in
        config.xml: SUNSET, which is what shipped, ICEBOX, EMBER and
        GRAPHITE. Any individual colour named in the file still wins
        over the theme, so you can take a palette and change one
        thing. If you are updating an older config.xml that spelled
        all thirteen colours out, delete those lines or the theme has
        nothing left to set.

    The instrument screen draws the FM routing. A box per operator,
        laid out by how far it is from the output, with a line from
        each one to whatever it feeds. The box is the level meter too,
        so an operator turned down is visibly an empty box; carriers
        are drawn in the highlight colour and modulators in the
        structure colour. It reads the same table the engine plays
        from, so the picture cannot drift from the sound.

    The phrase screen shows what just fired. A short bar beside every
        row that has a note and has just been passed, brightest on the
        row sounding now and fading over the three behind it. Gaps
        stay dark, runs light up in sequence, and the rhythm you are
        listening to is visible instead of inferred.

    The scope draws both channels, one trace above the other, same
        size. It was the left channel only, so a hard panned part or a
        ping pong delay looked exactly like a mono mix.

    The boot screen has the wordmark on it, for the few seconds the
        sample bank takes to open.

PSPATTERN 0.3.1a

    The DSP figure no longer shoots past 100 while rendering. It was
        not lying about the time, but it was calling it the wrong
        thing: the wav writer's flush happens inside the block being
        measured, because the tap that feeds it sits in the mixer's
        render. A 32KB write to a Memory Stick takes tens of
        milliseconds against a block budget of about six, so the block
        it lands on really did take that long -- and nothing was wrong,
        because the prebuffer covers it and the song plays straight
        through.

    A meter labelled dsp should say how close the DSP is to its
        deadline. Card I/O is not that, and showing it as if it were
        teaches people to ignore the number, which is worse than not
        having one. The writer times its own flush now and that time
        comes back off the total.

    Subtracted, not hidden: if a write ever gets slow enough to outlast
        the prebuffer, that is audible, and it is audible in the usual
        way rather than as a figure nobody trusts.

PSPATTERN 0.3.0a

    The SONG box on the project screen has five rows in it and was
        drawn four rows tall, so repeat sat on the bottom border
        instead of inside the box.

    Starting a render no longer stalls the song. The wav header was
        written as twelve separate calls -- four bytes, two bytes,
        four bytes -- and on a Memory Stick a write is a card
        transaction rather than a buffered nothing. Twelve of them
        landed at the instant the transport started. It is built in
        memory and written once now. The samples already went through
        a 32KB buffer; this is the one write that was still doing it
        the old way.

    Render Stems is gone. Eight files being written at once is 1.4MB
        a second to a card that does not want it in those pieces, and
        it never worked on hardware -- the render came out glitched,
        which is the worst way for a bounce to fail, because it looks
        like it worked until you listen to it. Stereo is unchanged.

    The send effects were rebuilt underneath, with no change to what
        they sound like. Everything the delay and reverb touch is one
        structure now rather than fifteen separate pieces of state,
        and it reads the knob positions as they were when the block
        started rather than reading them live -- so moving the reverb
        size while a block is being processed can no longer change the
        coefficients halfway through it.

    They can also run a block behind, which is off unless you set
        DEFERREDFX in config.xml. That exists for testing and there is
        no reason to turn it on otherwise: it costs a few milliseconds
        of latency on the wet signal and gives nothing back.

    Why any of that was done: the plan was to move the delay and
        reverb onto the PSP's second CPU, the Media Engine, which does
        nothing in a tracker and would have been worth about a third
        of a block. It is not happening. Half of the Media Engine core
        function table is unmapped on the PSP-1000 -- 37 of 75 entries
        against none on the slim and later -- so it would have worked
        on some machines and not others, and it needs a kernel module
        to do it.

    The bench measures the send bus as well as the voices, and
        docs/PERFORMANCE.md has the hardware figures, including the
        five things that were tried and did not work.

PSPATTERN 0.2.9a

    FM costs a sixth less than it did. Four operators at eight voices
        was 85% of one block on hardware and is 71%. Both halves of
        that came from measuring on a PSP; nothing on a build host or
        in an emulator could have shown either.

    The engine was not compute bound and not cache bound. It was
        spilling: 317 of its 547 memory operations were the compiler
        shuffling registers to the stack, because four operators of
        state do not fit in the register file. The operator loop is
        written out as scalars now instead of looping over arrays,
        which also drops three dead feedback tests, op0's routing
        (every algorithm sends it to the output) and op3's incoming
        modulation slot (nothing in any algorithm feeds op3, so it
        read a zero every sample).

    An integer division was running once per sample: the carrier sum
        divided by the carrier count. On this chip div is about
        thirty five cycles and does not pipeline. The count is 1 to 4
        and fixed for the whole render, so it is a reciprocal now.
        Negative sums can land one LSB lower than before.

    The CPU LOAD bench measures the send bus as well as the voices --
        delay, reverb, both, with the step axis reading as channels
        sending rather than voices sounding.

    Hardware figures are in docs/PERFORMANCE.md, including the four
        things that were tried and did not work. The emulator is 1.4
        to 1.8x optimistic on voices and 1.0 to 1.2x on the send bus.

PSPATTERN 0.2.8a

    The song screen has a memory bar where the dsp row was. The dsp
        figure was already on the title strip, so one of the five rows
        that panel has was spent saying something the screen already
        said. The overrun warning moved up there with it, and shows as
        dsp999! in the mute colour.

    Samples live in RAM because streaming them off the Memory Stick is
        not viable, so what is left is what decides whether the next
        one loads, and there was no way to find out until it did not.

    Two changes about where things sit relative to the PSP's 16KB data
        cache. Neither changes any arithmetic.

    The reverb's twenty four comb and allpass lines were twenty four
        separate allocations, so where they landed relative to each
        other was whatever the heap felt like that run. They are one
        block now, cache line aligned with a line of skew between
        them.

    The synth's lookup tables are 8.5KB against that same 16KB cache.
        They live in the PSP's scratchpad now: on chip, not cached,
        not competing with the audio for lines. Set SCRATCHPADTABLES
        to 0 in config.xml to put them back.

    Neither effect can be seen in an emulator, which models no cache.
        Watch the dsp figure on a reverb heavy song on hardware.

    sendfx_test existed and had never been wired up. It runs now, and
        under ASan.

PSPATTERN 0.2.7a

    Note ons are smoother. Three places where a parameter of 0 meant a
        step rather than the shortest fade:

    The sampler had no fade at all at its defaults. Attack 0, decay 0,
        full sustain, release 0 is what every plain sample uses, and
        that combination skipped the envelope entirely and started the
        note at full level in one sample. The step was the size of
        whatever the wav opened on. It fades now and then takes itself
        back out of the per-sample path, so a plain sample still costs
        nothing to play once the note is under way.

    Release 0 cut the voice where it stood, on both the sampler and the
        synth. Where one note is followed by the next, that cut lands
        on the same boundary as the note on, so it is heard as a click
        on the note coming in rather than on the one that ended. Both
        fade now.

    The shortest fade is 2.9ms rather than 1.45ms, and it is one
        constant used by attack, decay and release in both engines, so
        0 means the same thing everywhere. Still far too short to read
        as an attack or a release.

    A release tail is no longer mistaken for a sounding note. It was
        one flag answering two questions, and once every note had a
        tail the answers stopped agreeing: a note after silence glided
        up from the pitch of the note before it, and the FM operator
        envelopes carried over instead of starting again. The
        amplitude envelope still picks up from the tail, which is the
        point of it; nothing that shapes the timbre does.

PSPATTERN 0.2.6a

    R with a direction moves between screens and does nothing else.
        Everything that was held with R and was not navigation is held
        with L now, on every screen, the way the mixer already worked:
        mute, solo, and interpolate.

    The combos those collided with went the other way onto R: start a
        selection, clone, paste, cut, and widen. Nothing there can
        clash with the screen map, because a combo held with X or O
        never reaches the code that reads the map.

    On the mixer, the four settings of the send effects are walked with
        left and right. They sit side by side on one line and always
        did; reaching them took up and down. Up from that line returns
        to the channel strips.

    The version the program announces comes from the same place the
        release is named after. It had drifted to 0.2.0a while the
        builds went out as 0.2.5a.

    DEMO-3, a chiptune arrangement that leans on PDX. Not shipped in
        tester builds, which carry no songs at all.

PSPATTERN 0.2.5a

    The audio block was 128 frames, which gave the whole render 2.9ms
        to finish, 344 times a second. A block that overruns is filled
        with silence by the driver, and a run of those is a buzz at the
        block rate sitting on top of the music rather than a dropout
        anybody would recognise as one. That is the grit, and it is why
        it never appeared in the emulator: the arithmetic is identical
        there, only the time budget is not.
    Default is 256 now. Both the block size and the prebuffer count are
        settable in config.xml as AUDIOBUFFERSIZE and AUDIOPREBUFFER.
        If it is still gritty, try 512 and then 1024. Latency is frames
        times prebuffer over 44100.

    Muted channels no longer render. The instrument was rendered in
        full and the mute applied afterwards, so muting saved only the
        channel strip and soloing saved nothing at all. Soloing one
        channel to find out what was overloading the machine reported
        very nearly the load of the whole song.

    The DSP figure shows the worst block when one overruns, instead of
        an average smoothed over eight blocks and then clamped at 99. A
        machine can glitch steadily while an average sits at twenty.

    The channel strip is one pass instead of three. The high pass, the
        low pass and the fader each walked the whole block separately.

    The send path is 32 bit. Accumulate runs for every sample of every
        sending channel and was widening to 64 bit to multiply by a
        0..255 send. The delay line works in whole samples like the
        comb bank already did.

    PDX saw and square dropped a 64 bit multiply per sample that was
        never needed: the compare against the knee already bounds the
        product under 2^31. PDX is also in the load test now, which it
        was never in.

    The band limiting no longer divides per edge; the reciprocal moved
        to the pitch change.

    Wav writes are batched into 32 KB, which is why stems rendering
        broke up on hardware: eight files at 1.4 MB a second in blocks
        of a few hundred bytes is more than a Memory Stick will take.

PSPATTERN 0.2.4a

    No engine changes. Nothing under sources changed since 0.2.3a, so
        the program itself is identical and a tester who already has
        0.2.3a gains nothing by installing this. It is a version for
        the work around the program.

    A patch parameter the loader does not recognise is now a build
        error.
    InstrumentBank::RestoreContent applies a PARAM only where the name
        matches one of the instrument's variables exactly, and skips
        anything else without a word. Both demo songs had been setting
        "cutoff" and "reso" on every drum since they were written; a
        sample instrument's filter is "filter cut" and "filter res",
        and neither number ever reached the engine. The kit was
        balanced by ear against a patch that was ignoring two of its
        settings.
    The song builder now refuses to write a song carrying a parameter
        the loader would drop, and reads the valid names out of the C++
        rather than keeping a copy of them, so the check cannot drift
        from what it checks.
    The dead parameters are removed rather than renamed. Applying them
        now would be an untested change to two finished songs, and a
        4 kHz lowpass on the kick would take exactly the click it is
        supposed to keep. Removing a setting that never applied cannot
        change what either song sounds like.

    DEMO-2: the breakdown chord breathes.
    Row 5 held one strum a bar and then did nothing with it for four
        bars. A table now steps the volume down and back with the
        cutoff following, looping every twelve ticks, which at six
        ticks to a step is an eighth note, so it lands with the open
        hats. The amplitude envelope across that row moves through
        10.9 dB where it was flat. Not in a tester build, which ships
        no demo songs.

    Sampler measured, not yet fixed.
    The interpolator is healthy: at a ratio of 1.4983 it produces
        -76.1 dB of everything-that-is-not-the-fundamental against
        -76.5 dB for the same job done in floating point, so it is
        within half a decibel of what linear interpolation can do.
    Pitching a sample up is the gap. There is no filter before the
        decimation, so a band limited saw played at 1.4983x puts
        -26.5 dB of energy off the harmonic grid. That is worse than
        the oscillators were before they were band limited, and it is
        the next thing to fix.

PSPATTERN 0.2.3a

    FM: the operator sine is interpolated now.
    Every operator read the nearest of 1024 table entries and threw
        away the other 22 bits of phase. Measured on a bare operator
        with nothing modulating it, everything that was not the
        fundamental sat at -55 dB, which matched a no-interpolation
        reference to a tenth of a decibel.
    It matters more in FM than anywhere else in the synth, because a
        modulator's impurity is multiplied into the carrier's spectrum
        rather than just added to the output. Two operator FM had
        -44 dB of energy sitting between the partials where the Bessel
        spectrum says there should be none.
    Now interpolated against a step table, the way msfa does it, so the
        lookup is a multiply and an add. Bare operator -89 dB, two
        operator FM -77 dB between the partials: 34 dB and 33 dB
        better. Costs 4 points at eight voices of four operator FM,
        47 to 51 per cent of a block.

    FM filter fix, a regression from 0.2.2a.
    That release made the SVF tick twice per sample in the TONE and VAX
        engines and rebuilt the shared coefficient table for the
        doubled rate. The FM engine has the same filter and was missed,
        so it was reading half rate coefficients into a single pass and
        every FM patch's filter sat an octave below where its cutoff
        said it was. All three engines tick twice now.

PSPATTERN 0.2.2a

    The oscillators are band limited now.
    Saw and pulse were naive: they stepped from one sample to the next
        without regard for where inside the sample the edge fell, which
        scatters energy off the harmonics and across the spectrum.
        Measured on a rendered note, energy at frequencies that are not
        in the note ran at -30 dB at A2 and -16 dB at A5, worsening
        3 dB an octave upward. That matched a naive reference exactly,
        which is what confirmed there was no band limiting at all.
    Now a two sample PolyBLEP residual at each discontinuity, on the
        main oscillator, on every member of the unison stack and on the
        sub. Same measurement after: -67 dB at A2 and -32 dB at A5, so
        16 dB better across the range and 37 dB at the bottom.
    The divide only runs inside the edge window, which is twice a
        cycle. Cost at eight voices: tone 17 to 18 per cent of a
        block, vax 25 to 30 with the filter change below.

    The filter is a real filter across its whole range.
    A Chamberlin SVF is only stable while its coefficient stays well
        under 2, which capped the table at 7.6 kHz and then clamped.
        A clamped coefficient is not a corner frequency: measured,
        cutoff 255 with resonance was not a 7.6 kHz lowpass but nearly
        flat to 15 kHz, with the warp near the stability limit standing
        in for an open filter. The top third of the knob did almost
        nothing.
    The SVF now ticks twice per sample, which halves the rate it sees
        and buys the headroom to make the range real. Table extended to
        30 Hz - 12.5 kHz. Measured after: cutoff 255 is -3 dB at
        15 kHz, cutoff 192 at 3520 Hz with -32.9 dB at 10 kHz where it
        used to be -21.2, and no resonant hump.
    Cutoff numbers now mean different frequencies than they did. Every
        setting is somewhat brighter than before, which is the safer
        direction, but existing patches are worth a listen.

    DEMO-2 level 0.29 to 0.26. A PolyBLEP residual overshoots slightly
        at each edge and the filter passes more top, which together
        took the render from no clipped samples to 84. Now 11 in 5.7
        million, all of them transient peaks in the four full
        arrangement rows.

PSPATTERN 0.2.1a

    Rendering to wav did not stop when the song stopped.
    Tearing the render down lived in the song screen's START handler, so
        it only ran when somebody pressed START a second time. A song
        that reaches its end with repeat on "once" stops on its own and
        never went through it, so the screen went on saying it was
        rendering over a file the mixer had already closed.
    Worse, the mixer stayed in stereo render mode, so the next START
        opened a fresh writer on the same path. Opening truncates, so a
        finished render was replaced by however much of the new take had
        been written. A 65 second wav came back as 0.78 seconds.
    Now handled where the player's stop event arrives, so it works from
        any screen, and it forces a redraw: a notification is painted
        into the character grid, and once playback stops nothing repaints
        the row it sits on.

    Changing an instrument's type asked "settings are lost" on
        instruments that had no settings.
    The prompt asked IsEmpty, which is the question the save code asks.
        Synth and midi instruments answer "never empty" there because
        they always have to be written to the file, so both always
        warned, including a patch straight out of the box. New projects
        assign a drum to the first instruments automatically, so those
        warned too.
    There is now a separate question for it: has anything been changed
        from the values a new instrument of this type starts with. The
        sample a new project assigns for you and the midi channel the
        bank stamps in by index are not counted, because neither was
        chosen by anybody.

    DEMO-2, a second demo song. Melody led rather than arpeggio led,
        with one arp channel instead of three, and a chord channel that
        runs the same ARPG command at a slower ARPS so it strums instead
        of trilling. Peak -0.09 dBFS, no clipped samples.

PSPATTERN 0.2.0a

    Engine fixes:
    A note that followed another note on the same channel was thrown
        away one audio block after it started. PlayerChannel set its
        releasing_ flag when stopping the old note and nothing cleared
        it, so the next Render read it as "the release finished" and
        dropped the instrument. Every note after the first on any patch
        with a release, cut about 20ms in.
    Note-on declick used the wrong correction. It added the previous
        sample instead of measuring the step, which for a square left
        nearly a full scale jump. Measured at 18.4% of full scale on a
        square, 16.6% on a pulse; now 0.00% on both.
    The declick bound could not cancel the step it measured. Capped at
        half full scale while a loud square swings 57%.
    Songs no longer loop by default.
    The low battery warning inverted once per repaint on a panel that
        repaints every frame, so it strobed instead of blinking. Now on
        the wall clock at 2Hz, with hysteresis on the threshold.
    The project selector listed the program's own samplelib folder.

    Additions:
    Velocity column in the phrase screen, reading 00..99 rather than
        hex. Empty means full, so older projects are unchanged.
    Hard sync and ring modulation on the VAX engine.
    ARPS: arp speed, in ticks per arpeggio position. Default 1 is the
        classic every-tick trill; higher values slow the figure until
        each note in it can be heard as a pitch.
    MANUAL.txt.
    Channel faders are written by the song generators, so a demo can
        carry its own mix balance.

    Interface:
    Instrument values read as decibels, per cent and L/CTR/R instead of
        raw hex where the raw number meant nothing.
    Four strings named buttons the PSP does not have.
    The boot and browser screens named the upstream project.


    Migrate W32 to SDL2, removing need for building in vs2008

